###############################################################################
# Q11 -- Computational resources
###############################################################################

# 3BPA models: one NVIDIA RTX 4090. Universal models: eight NVIDIA H100
# GPUs, about 960 hours in total for the OAM and OMAT models (whether
# wall-clock or GPU hours is not stated, so it is kept as text). Table 4
# gives MD speed in ps/day for several universal models and system sizes.

###############################################################################
# Q12 -- Gaps and ambiguities (prose; not triples)
###############################################################################

# - cNequIP and cAllegro are the same architectures as NequIP and Allegro with the tensor algebra in irreducible Cartesian form (same parameter count, per the paper) and are encoded under the same registry methods with a 'tensor-basis' setting. The authors note that only one random seed was run.
# - Table 1 has six model sizes and a dihedral-scan test; one size per architecture and the three temperature tests are encoded. Table 2 (MACE and cMACE) and the appendix tables were not read.
# - The universal model's benchmark results (Matbench Discovery F1 and related scores) have no MetricProperty. Its settings are those of the OMat24 pretraining column of Table 8 (correlation order 2 as bodyOrder, 48 channels as numFeatures); the fine-tuning stage is in the algorithm label.
# - TACE is a new registry method first used here; its method paper is cited as Xu et al. 2026. Preprint only (arXiv v2); authors from DataCite, without ORCIDs.
# - Author affiliations are not encoded (ORCIDs are those deposited with the DOI metadata, source: datacite).
